. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * Please add a verbal description of your model here
. 
.         /*      
>         We construct the outcome variable, gender_equality, by standardizing 
> responses (mean = 0, standard deviation = 1) to survey question plh0308_v2, w
> hich asks respondents the extent to which they agree with the statement: "Bes
> t if man and woman work the same amount so they can share the responsibility.
> " Higher values of gender_equality thus indicate stronger support for gender 
> equality in labor participation.
> 
>         We estimate a linear regression model where the primary independent v
> ariable is an indicator for whether the respondent has a daughter. The specif
> ication controls for a rich set of covariates: respondent's gender, age, inco
> me, years of education, nationality, marital status, survey year, and the age
>  of the child. The coefficient on the daughter variable is interpreted as the
>  marginal effect of having a daughter (versus a son) on support for gender eq
> uality, expressed in standard deviation units.
> 
>         The sample is restricted to individuals with exactly one child. Since
>  the outcome variable is only available in the 2018 wave, this restriction en
> sures that there is a single observation per respondent. To minimize child's 
> gender selection, we exclude homosexuals.
>         */
. 
.         global controls_h1 "years_edu male age age_child married ln_income i.
> syear i.nationality" 

.         
.         *h1 -> only 2018
.         reg gender_equality daughter $controls_h1  if sample==1  , robust
note: 2018.syear omitted because of collinearity.

